`,e.jsx(q,{items:[{q:"Why does logistic regression fail on XOR while a depth-2 tree solves it exactly?",a:"XOR needs class 1 when both features are high OR both low, which no single hyperplane can separate — logistic regression scores about 50%. A tree makes a decision CONDITIONAL on an earlier decision: split on x1, then split on x2 differently in each branch. Two axis-aligned cuts carve out the four quadrants exactly."},{q:"State what a trained tree is, mathematically.",a:"A piecewise-constant function: the K leaves partition feature space into K disjoint axis-aligned rectangles, and the prediction is a fixed value c_k inside each. Everything about trees follows from this — the staircase boundaries, the inability to extrapolate, and the interpretability."},{q:"An applicant is approved and credit_score never entered the decision. Bug?",a:"No. Once a sample routes into a subtree, only that subtree's splits apply, so the features consulted are path-dependent. Credit score may be decisive for low-income applicants and irrelevant for high-income ones — a form of automatic, locally adaptive feature selection linear models cannot express."},{q:"Why is a tree's inference cost independent of the number of features?",a:"Prediction evaluates one comparison per level along a single root-to-leaf path, so the cost is O(depth) — typically O(log n) for a balanced tree. Features that appear in no split on that path are never touched, regardless of how many exist in the data."},{q:"In the regression example, adding a split made one leaf fit its single point perfectly. What is that the start of?",a:"Overfitting. A leaf containing one training point has zero error on that point by construction, but its prediction is that point's noise as much as its signal. Continue and you reach one leaf per sample — a lookup table with zero training error and no generalisation."}]}),`
